---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const gbufferAttachments = [
  { id: "Attachment 0", desc: "Depth" },
  { id: "Attachment 1", desc: "Surface Normals" },
  { id: "Attachment 2", desc: "Albedo (RGB) + Metalness (A)" },
  { id: "Attachment 3", desc: "Emissive (RGB) + Roughness (A)" },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Vulkan Renderer | tRizyal"
  backHref="/#projects"
  backLabel="Back to Projects"
>
  <!-- Title & Metadata -->
  <header class="space-y-4">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300">
      <span class="flex items-center gap-1">
        <i
          class="w-3.5 h-3.5 svg-icon"
          style="-webkit-mask-image: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/simple-icons@v11/icons/cplusplus.svg'); mask-image: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/simple-icons@v11/icons/cplusplus.svg');"
        ></i>{" "}
        C++
      </span>
      <span class="text-slate-400 dark:text-slate-600">&bull;</span>
      <span class="flex items-center gap-1">
        <i
          class="w-3.5 h-3.5 svg-icon"
          style="-webkit-mask-image: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/simple-icons@v11/icons/vulkan.svg'); mask-image: url('https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/simple-icons@v11/icons/vulkan.svg');"
        ></i>{" "}
        VULKAN
      </span>
      <span class="text-slate-400 dark:text-slate-600">&bull;</span>
      <span>GLSL</span>
      <span class="text-slate-400 dark:text-slate-600">&bull;</span>
      <span>GLFW</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-fascinate tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
      Vulkan Renderer
    </h1>

    <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
      A modular real-time rendering application built from the ground up in
      Vulkan, featuring toggleable shading pipelines, Cook-Torrance PBR,
      G-buffer deferred rendering, post-processing, and diagnostic visualization
      modes.
    </p>

    <div class="pt-1 font-mono">
      <a
        href="https://github.com/trizyal/VulkanRasterisation2"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 font-mono text-xs px-4 py-2.5 rounded-lg border border-accent/40 bg-accent/10 text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300 hover:bg-accent/20 transition-all shadow"
      >
        View on GitHub &nearr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- Video Showcase -->
  <section class="viewport-frame rounded-2xl overflow-hidden bg-slate-900 dark:bg-black border border-slate-300 dark:border-white/10 aspect-video flex items-center justify-center">
    <video
      controls
      autoplay
      muted
      loop
      playsinline
      class="w-full h-auto block opacity-95 dark:opacity-100"
    >
      <source src="/vulkan/vulkan.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
      Your browser does not support the video tag.
    </video>
  </section>

  <!-- Technical Breakdown -->
  <article class="viewport-frame rounded-2xl bg-white/60 dark:bg-surface/50 backdrop-blur-md p-6 sm:p-10 space-y-8 leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
    <section class="space-y-3">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-accent tracking-widest uppercase">
        01 // Core Engine &amp; Infrastructure
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-grotesk font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
        Vulkan Initialization &amp; Windowing
      </h2>
      <p>
        As my first major project in Vulkan, I built the entire real-time
        rendering architecture from scratch. This involved initializing the
        Vulkan instance with validation layers, configuring window surface
        integration, selecting physical and logical devices, and managing the
        swap chain, render passes, and framebuffers. For scene navigation, I
        implemented an interactive camera system using GLFW’s event-driven
        callback and input polling framework.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-accent tracking-widest uppercase">
        02 // Base Rendering &amp; Diagnostics
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-grotesk font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
        Forward Base Pass &amp; Debug Visualizations
      </h2>
      <p>
        I began with a base-color forward pipeline to verify mesh loading and
        texture sampling across the scene. To validate geometry and sampling
        behavior before introducing complex lighting, I built toggleable debug
        visualization pipelines for inspecting{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          texture mipmap levels
        </strong>
        ,{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">surface normals</strong>,{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">fragment depth</strong>,
        and{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          partial depth derivatives
        </strong>{" "}
        (
        <code class="font-mono text-xs text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300">
          dFdx
        </code>{" "}
        /{" "}
        <code class="font-mono text-xs text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300">
          dFdy
        </code>
        ).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-accent tracking-widest uppercase">
        03 // Physically Based Rendering
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-grotesk font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
        Cook-Torrance BRDF &amp; Alpha Masking
      </h2>
      <p>
        To achieve physically plausible materials, I configured uniform buffers
        and descriptor sets to stream per-frame and per-material data to the
        shaders. The lighting model implements the{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Cook-Torrance microfacet BRDF
        </strong>
        —combining a{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Beckmann normal distribution function (NDF)
        </strong>
        ,{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Schlick’s Fresnel approximation
        </strong>
        , and a{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Lambertian diffuse
        </strong>{" "}
        term. Transparent and alpha-masked geometry is isolated into a dedicated
        pipeline pass rendered after opaque meshes to preserve correct sorting
        and blending.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-accent tracking-widest uppercase">
        04 // Post-Processing &amp; Synchronization
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-grotesk font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
        Render-to-Texture Pixelation Filter
      </h2>
      <p>
        I added a render-to-texture post-processing pass that applies a{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          5 &times; 3 texel pixelation filter
        </strong>{" "}
        to the final scene output. Implementing this cleanly required explicit
        pipeline synchronization, using subpass dependencies and image layout
        transitions to prevent read-after-write hazards between rendering
        passes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-3">
      <div class="font-mono text-xs text-accent tracking-widest uppercase">
        05 // Deferred Shading Pipeline
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-grotesk font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
        4-Attachment G-Buffer &amp; Over-Shading Reduction
      </h2>
      <p>
        To eliminate redundant fragment lighting calculations on occluded
        geometry, I transitioned the engine toward deferred shading. First, I
        implemented an{" "}
        <strong class="text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          overdraw and over-shading debug view
        </strong>{" "}
        to profile pixel-shading bottlenecks. I then constructed a multi-target
        G-buffer render pass with four attachments:
      </p>
      <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-3 pt-1 font-mono text-xs">
        {gbufferAttachments.map((item) => (
          <li class="p-3.5 rounded-xl bg-slate-100 dark:bg-obsidian/80 border border-slate-200 dark:border-white/10">
            <span class="text-cyan-700 dark:text-cyan-300 font-bold">
              {item.id}:
            </span>{" "}
            <span class="text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">{item.desc}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p>
        In the subsequent lighting pass, these packed G-buffer attachments are
        sampled in screen space so PBR lighting is evaluated strictly once per
        visible pixel—significantly reducing over-shading and improving total
        frame lighting times.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</ArticleLayout>
